{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019od6cVeZXXC6PPFYz8xtB8","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"commits\":[{\"hash\":\"5eb8cba2e30befeaec8ab7892dff14ac7dcc9d87\",\"date\":\"2026-10-13T17:37:18+03:00\",\"message\":\"TRUNK-6823: Deny with APIAuthenticationException again, as an AuthorizationDeniedException (#6674)\",\"refs\":\"HEAD, origin/master, origin/HEAD, master\",\"body\":\"* TRUNK-6823: Deny with APIAuthenticationException again, as an AuthorizationDeniedException\\n\\nSince TRUNK-6799, AuthorizationAdvice and core's explicit privilege\\nchecks denied with Spring Security's AccessDeniedException rather than\\nAPIAuthenticationException. Code catching the old type stopped\\nrecognizing denials; webservices.rest maps only that type, so REST\\nanswered 500 where it answered 401 or 403.\\n\\nAPIAuthenticationException now extends AuthorizationDeniedException.\\nCore throws it again wherever it did before 3.0.0, and wherever a\\n@PreAuthorize or @PostAuthorize check names the missing privilege, so a\\ncatch on either type recognizes the denial. It stays deprecated in\\nfavour of AccessDeniedException, which every denial is.\\n\\nIt is no longer an APIException, so a module compiled against an earlier\\ncore whose bytecode treats it as one fails verification until rebuilt.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WMHrjRkMgotZngQzt31zSK\\n\\n* TRUNK-6823: Deny a failed isAuthenticated() with APIAuthenticationException too\\n\\n@PreAuthorize(\\\"isAuthenticated()\\\"), the migration guide's translation of a\\nno-value @Authorized, denied a logged-out caller with a plain\\nAuthorizationDeniedException where @Authorized throws\\nAPIAuthenticationException, so converting such a method changed what its\\ncallers catch. It now records the denial for\\nPrivilegeNamingAuthorizationManager to report as AuthorizationAdvice does.\\n\\nTestInstallUtil throws APIAuthenticationException again, as before 3.0.0,\\nand the migration guide notes that the class is no longer an APIException.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WMHrjRkMgotZngQzt31zSK\\n\\n---------\\n\\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"authorName\":\"dkayiwa\",\"authorEmail\":\"dkayiwa@openmrs.org\"},{\"hash\":\"a69b14a84bf925ff7cdf34e93ca1ffb4f257265f\",\"date\":\"2026-10-08T15:44:45+02:00\",\"message\":\"TRUNK-6799 Introduce Spring Security with support for legacy authentication context (#6544)\",\"refs\":\"\",\"body\":\"Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"authorName\":\"Rafal Korytkowski\",\"authorEmail\":\"rafal.korytkowski@gmail.com\"},{\"hash\":\"f5c03501860de4372d7e0e12f9c3dbe025a38dc8\",\"date\":\"2026-06-18T17:08:02+03:00\",\"message\":\"Require Get Alerts privilege to read all users' alerts (#6186)\",\"refs\":\"\",\"body\":\"* Require Get Alerts privilege to read all users' alerts\\n\\nAlertService.getAllAlerts() and getAllAlerts(boolean) return every user's\\nalerts, but were guarded only by @Authorized (authentication), so any\\nauthenticated user could read alerts addressed to others through any consumer\\n(REST, legacy UI, modules, direct API).\\n\\nIntroduce a dedicated Get Alerts privilege (following the GET_* read-privilege\\nconvention) and gate both methods with it. The per-user reads (getAlerts,\\ngetAlertsByUser, getAllActiveAlerts) stay open so a user can still read their\\nown alerts. The privilege is created on startup via @AddOnStartup and is not\\nauto-granted to any role, so this does not reintroduce the leak.\\n\\nAlertReminderTask reads all alerts as its configured (possibly unprivileged)\\nscheduled-job user, so it grants itself a proxy Get Alerts privilege for that\\nread.\\n\\nUpgrade note: existing roles that relied on Manage Alerts to list alerts must\\nbe granted the new Get Alerts privilege (super users are unaffected).\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\\n\\n* Tighten cross-user alert reads on getAlert and getAlerts\\n\\ngetAllAlerts() is gated by Get Alerts, but getAlert(Integer),\\ngetAlerts(User, ...), getAlertsByUser(User) and getAllActiveAlerts(User)\\nstill let any authenticated caller read another user's alerts by passing\\nsomeone else's id/User. Guard them in the service layer: a caller may read\\ntheir own alerts, and reading another user's alerts requires the Get Alerts\\nprivilege. getAlertsByUser and getAllActiveAlerts delegate through the\\nguarded getAlerts; the self check compares userId, the same key the query\\nfilters on.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\n\\n* Return null from getAlert for another user's alert instead of throwing\\n\\ngetAlert(Integer) threw APIAuthenticationException when an alert existed but was addressed to another user, yet returned null for a non-existent id - letting an authenticated caller distinguish the two and probe which alert ids exist. It now returns null in both cases so the lookup cannot be used as an existence oracle. Reading one's own alert, and privileged reads via the Get Alerts privilege, are unchanged.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\n\\n---------\\n\\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"authorName\":\"dkayiwa\",\"authorEmail\":\"dkayiwa@openmrs.org\"}],\"totalCount\":3}"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ae3967b-799a-400b-b888-5a68c6030011","uuid":"52acf1c1-76bd-4924-b118-80e4bf293283","timestamp":"2026-10-23T05:44:39.965Z","tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"commits\":[{\"hash\":\"5eb8cba2e30befeaec8ab7892dff14ac7dcc9d87\",\"date\":\"2026-10-13T17:37:18+03:00\",\"message\":\"TRUNK-6823: Deny with APIAuthenticationException again, as an AuthorizationDeniedException (#6674)\",\"refs\":\"HEAD, origin/master, origin/HEAD, master\",\"body\":\"* TRUNK-6823: Deny with APIAuthenticationException again, as an AuthorizationDeniedException\\n\\nSince TRUNK-6799, AuthorizationAdvice and core's explicit privilege\\nchecks denied with Spring Security's AccessDeniedException rather than\\nAPIAuthenticationException. Code catching the old type stopped\\nrecognizing denials; webservices.rest maps only that type, so REST\\nanswered 500 where it answered 401 or 403.\\n\\nAPIAuthenticationException now extends AuthorizationDeniedException.\\nCore throws it again wherever it did before 3.0.0, and wherever a\\n@PreAuthorize or @PostAuthorize check names the missing privilege, so a\\ncatch on either type recognizes the denial. It stays deprecated in\\nfavour of AccessDeniedException, which every denial is.\\n\\nIt is no longer an APIException, so a module compiled against an earlier\\ncore whose bytecode treats it as one fails verification until rebuilt.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WMHrjRkMgotZngQzt31zSK\\n\\n* TRUNK-6823: Deny a failed isAuthenticated() with APIAuthenticationException too\\n\\n@PreAuthorize(\\\"isAuthenticated()\\\"), the migration guide's translation of a\\nno-value @Authorized, denied a logged-out caller with a plain\\nAuthorizationDeniedException where @Authorized throws\\nAPIAuthenticationException, so converting such a method changed what its\\ncallers catch. It now records the denial for\\nPrivilegeNamingAuthorizationManager to report as AuthorizationAdvice does.\\n\\nTestInstallUtil throws APIAuthenticationException again, as before 3.0.0,\\nand the migration guide notes that the class is no longer an APIException.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WMHrjRkMgotZngQzt31zSK\\n\\n---------\\n\\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"authorName\":\"dkayiwa\",\"authorEmail\":\"dkayiwa@openmrs.org\"},{\"hash\":\"a69b14a84bf925ff7cdf34e93ca1ffb4f257265f\",\"date\":\"2026-10-08T15:44:45+02:00\",\"message\":\"TRUNK-6799 Introduce Spring Security with support for legacy authentication context (#6544)\",\"refs\":\"\",\"body\":\"Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"authorName\":\"Rafal Korytkowski\",\"authorEmail\":\"rafal.korytkowski@gmail.com\"},{\"hash\":\"f5c03501860de4372d7e0e12f9c3dbe025a38dc8\",\"date\":\"2026-06-18T17:08:02+03:00\",\"message\":\"Require Get Alerts privilege to read all users' alerts (#6186)\",\"refs\":\"\",\"body\":\"* Require Get Alerts privilege to read all users' alerts\\n\\nAlertService.getAllAlerts() and getAllAlerts(boolean) return every user's\\nalerts, but were guarded only by @Authorized (authentication), so any\\nauthenticated user could read alerts addressed to others through any consumer\\n(REST, legacy UI, modules, direct API).\\n\\nIntroduce a dedicated Get Alerts privilege (following the GET_* read-privilege\\nconvention) and gate both methods with it. The per-user reads (getAlerts,\\ngetAlertsByUser, getAllActiveAlerts) stay open so a user can still read their\\nown alerts. The privilege is created on startup via @AddOnStartup and is not\\nauto-granted to any role, so this does not reintroduce the leak.\\n\\nAlertReminderTask reads all alerts as its configured (possibly unprivileged)\\nscheduled-job user, so it grants itself a proxy Get Alerts privilege for that\\nread.\\n\\nUpgrade note: existing roles that relied on Manage Alerts to list alerts must\\nbe granted the new Get Alerts privilege (super users are unaffected).\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\\n\\n* Tighten cross-user alert reads on getAlert and getAlerts\\n\\ngetAllAlerts() is gated by Get Alerts, but getAlert(Integer),\\ngetAlerts(User, ...), getAlertsByUser(User) and getAllActiveAlerts(User)\\nstill let any authenticated caller read another user's alerts by passing\\nsomeone else's id/User. Guard them in the service layer: a caller may read\\ntheir own alerts, and reading another user's alerts requires the Get Alerts\\nprivilege. getAlertsByUser and getAllActiveAlerts delegate through the\\nguarded getAlerts; the self check compares userId, the same key the query\\nfilters on.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\n\\n* Return null from getAlert for another user's alert instead of throwing\\n\\ngetAlert(Integer) threw APIAuthenticationException when an alert existed but was addressed to another user, yet returned null for a non-existent id - letting an authenticated caller distinguish the two and probe which alert ids exist. It now returns null in both cases so the lookup cannot be used as an existence oracle. Reading one's own alert, and privileged reads via the Get Alerts privilege, are unchanged.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\n\\n---------\\n\\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"authorName\":\"dkayiwa\",\"authorEmail\":\"dkayiwa@openmrs.org\"}],\"totalCount\":3}"}],"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019od6cVeZXXC6PPFYz8xtB8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
